{"name":"Elemental Tan","hex":"#cab79c","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"elemental","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of, relating to, or being an element (as opposed to a compound).","Basic, fundamental or elementary."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A creature (usually a spirit) that is attuned with, or composed of, one of the classical elements: air, earth, fire and water or variations of them like ice, lightning, etc. They sometimes have unique proper names and sometimes are referred to as Air, Earth, Fire, or Water."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/elemental"},{"term":"tan","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["The trigonometric function tangent."]},{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Tangale."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/tan"}],"entries":[{"title":"Elemental","blocks":[{"text":"An elemental is a mythic supernatural being that is described in occult and alchemical works from around the time of the European Renaissance, and particularly elaborated in the 16th century works of Paracelsus. According to Paracelsus and his subsequent followers, there are four categories of elementals, which are gnomes, undines, sylphs, and salamanders. These correspond to the four Empedoclean elements of antiquity: earth, water, air, and fire, respectively. Terms employed for beings associated with alchemical elements vary by source and gloss."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/Undine_Rising_from_the_Waters%2C_front.JPG/330px-Undine_Rising_from_the_Waters%2C_front.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elemental"},{"title":"Tan (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Tan is a pale tone of brown. The name is derived from tannum used in the tanning of leather."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Chestnut_Oak_Bark.jpg/330px-Chestnut_Oak_Bark.jpg?u